# Releasing

Two independent version concepts — don't conflate them:

- **Mod semver** (`modversion=X.Y.Z` in both `mod.info` files) — bumped every release, must
  equal the git tag `vX.Y.Z`. `tooling/scripts/package.*` enforce tag == modversion.
- **Targeted PZ build** (`name=__MOD_NAME__ (B42.x)` in `42/mod.info`, and any
  `title=... [B42.x+]` line in `workshop/workshop.vdf`) — changed only when you retarget a
  new game build. That's [UPDATING-PZ.md](UPDATING-PZ.md), not a normal release.

## Release checklist

1. **Bump** the mod semver:
   ```
   mise run bump 1.3.14
   ```
2. **Verify**: `mise run check`, then in-game SP + MP per [TESTING.md](TESTING.md).
3. **Update `workshop/workshop.vdf`** (title / description / tags) if user-facing behavior
   changed.
4. **Commit** the version bump + changes.
5. **Package sanity** (optional, CI also does this): `mise run package v1.3.14` builds
   `dist/__MOD_NAME__` and fails on any version mismatch.
   > `package` is the shared build step — CI, `deploy`, and `publish` all go through it. It
   > has **twin** implementations, `tooling/scripts/package.ps1` (local dev) and
   > `tooling/scripts/package.sh` (CI-only); both live in the shared `tooling/` submodule,
   > so fixes to one land in every mod repo together.
6. **Tag + GitHub release**: run the **Release Mod** GitHub Action
   (`.github/workflows/release.yml`, `workflow_dispatch`) with tag `v1.3.14`.
7. **Publish to Steam Workshop** (local, interactive). The target is **required** — publish
   to the **test** item, verify its page, *then* **prod**:
   ```
   mise run publish test "Short changenote"   # verify at the test item URL first
   mise run publish prod "Short changenote"   # only once test looks right
   ```

## Steam Workshop publish (`tooling/scripts/publish-workshop.{ps1,sh}`)

- **One-time:** `cp mise.local.toml.example mise.local.toml` and set `STEAM_USERNAME` +
  `STEAM_PASSWORD`. `mise run publish` logs in non-interactively.
- **One-time:** fill in `workshop/item-ids.json` with your Workshop `test`/`prod` item ids
  (public, not secret — safe to commit).
- **Target is required and explicit.** `mise run publish <test|prod> "note"` — there is **no
  default and no env fallback**, so you can't publish to prod by accident.
- Requires **steamcmd** on PATH (`winget install Valve.SteamCMD` on Windows), plus **`jq`**
  on Linux/macOS (used to read `workshop/item-ids.json`; Windows reads it via PowerShell's
  own JSON support, no extra tool needed).
- It rebuilds `dist/__MOD_NAME__`, fills `workshop/workshop.vdf` into
  `.publish/workshop.vdf`, then runs
  `steamcmd +login <user> +workshop_build_item <vdf> +quit`.
- **`workshop/workshop.vdf` is the source of truth for the Workshop page** — a steamcmd
  KeyValues file stored **verbatim**. The script only substitutes the dynamic fields:
  `{{PUBLISHEDFILEID}}`, `{{VISIBILITY}}` (per target — prod public, test unlisted),
  `{{CONTENTFOLDER}}`, `{{PREVIEWFILE}}`, `{{CHANGENOTE}}`. Edit `workshop/workshop.vdf` and
  re-publish to change the page — don't edit it in-browser (a publish overwrites it).
- **Preview it first.** `pwsh -File tooling/scripts/publish-workshop.ps1 test "note" -DryRun`
  (or `bash tooling/scripts/publish-workshop.sh test "note" --dry-run` on Linux/macOS) builds
  + prints the VDF **without uploading** (works without steamcmd installed).
- Set the Steam user via `-SteamUser`/`--steam-user` or `$env:STEAM_USERNAME`/`$STEAM_USERNAME`;
  otherwise it prompts.
- **Caveat:** only the single **preview** image is manageable via steamcmd. Extra gallery
  screenshots aren't settable this way — put images that should live in the description as
  `[img]<url>[/img]` BBCode instead.

steamcmd is the only publish path — the in-game uploader reads a `workshop.txt` this repo
doesn't ship, since page metadata lives in source-controlled `workshop/workshop.vdf` instead.

## After publishing

- Confirm the item at `https://steamcommunity.com/sharedfiles/filedetails/?id=<your-prod-id>`.
- The GitHub release zip is the offline/manual-install artifact.
